There you go. We're good.

23
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
So your question in terms of how I personally became interested in crypto.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:02,000
So my entire 20 year career has always been in IT technology, servers, security networks, data center infrastructure, etc.

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
So I've always been a techie at heart.

26
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Then about nine years ago, I got really into personal investing and did a lot to educate myself, write a lot of books,

27
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
came across the concept of diversification and alternative assets.

28
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
And somehow I stumbled upon Bitcoin.

29
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
I actually don't remember exactly where.

30
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
And at first, like many others, I didn't really understand it.

31
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
So I went deep down that rabbit hole, kept asking why.

32
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Next thing you know, I'm teaching myself about the merits of Austrian economics.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
And like many others who go so deep, they basically never come out of that rabbit hole and they're hooked for a life.

34
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
This was exacerbated in 2020 during DeFi summer with the magic of interacting with protocols like Aave and Uniswap.

35
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:53,000
And eventually I made the switch to work full time in crypto almost exactly three years ago when I joined KPMG and their innovation function as a technical blockchain subject matter expert.

36
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,000
So you had a deep interest in crypto and joined KPMG on the back of that interest to be clear.

37
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Exactly.

38
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
And can you speak to KPMG's history of being active in the crypto space over that time window and beyond?

39
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Actually, as far back as 2017, KPMG became one of the first public accounting firms to audit digital assets held by public companies.

40
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:22,000
We pioneered tools and methodologies for digital asset audits, including becoming a founding member of the AICPA's digital assets working group.

41
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:31,000
And that went to Overdrive in 2020 when a large audit client originated the Bitcoin treasury strategy and we have served them every step of the way since then.

42
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,000
And that opened the floodgates. We onboarded many clients in the crypto space in the years following that.

43
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Funds, ETF issuers, infrastructure companies, Bitcoin miners, a major stablecoin issuer and some of the largest operators of Ethereum and Solana validators and many more.

44
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
You touched on a lot of interesting topics.

45
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Obviously, the reserve strategy is topical.

46
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
In that respect, today, how are you and the firm involved in the industry?

47
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
And feel free to take that any direction that makes sense.

48
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Obviously, you touched on a number of interesting categories.

49
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Well, we've been quite busy.

50
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:17,000
So we work closely with regulatory and standard setting entities such as the AICPA, FASB, the SEC, etc., where we discuss the digital asset landscape.

51
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
We became one of the first approved firms to provide attestation services to the node operator risk standard industry forum.

52
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
And we were actually looking into other types of crypto industry standards engagements as well.

53
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
We've done deal advisory work for some of the largest M&A activities you've seen over the last year in the crypto space.

54
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,000
We've completed risk assessment work for a major payments company launching a stablecoin compliance work for some of the spot ETFs.

55
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:48,000
And we recently started issuing examination reports for issued stablecoins and their related reserves as of a few months ago.

56
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
This is on top of the fact that we continue to invest in our tools and our technology processes,

57
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
specifically our patented and proprietary chain fusion tool, which allows us to utilize on-chain data in audit evidence.

58
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,000
There is a structured and managed approach that's needed when dealing with on-chain data in audits, and that is where our tool chain fusion comes in.

59
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
That's quite a scope.

60
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:16,000
I think a lot of people can get lost in the types of activity that everyone sees in the scheme of crypto companies being investment and payments,

61
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
but you forget about all the other needs there.

62
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
What sorts of firms are you working with when it comes to CFI, DeFi funds?

63
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
You have a lot of structures in the ecosystem.

64
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
So really all kinds of firms and sizes.

65
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:37,000
We've done from billion-dollar funds and billion-dollar acquisitions all the way down to helping small DAOs with some tax work related to their tokens.

66
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
So we've done complicated tax works and tax work involving chain mergers, token migrations, protocol upgrades,

67
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
and other types of on-chain M&A work.

68
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,000
At the end of the day, there are a variety of regulatory and legal needs to operate companies and participate in certain transactions in the United States,

69
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:01,000
whether it's folks needing help with tax or audit, or they need help with their money transmitter licenses, building out risk controls,

70
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:10,000
SOC reports, AML KYC programs, applying for bank charters, IP ownership and transfer issues, or other matters.

71
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
That's interesting, especially in the scope of DAOs.

72
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
I think we've seen the playbook being written in terms of how DAOs should operate in real time.

73
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:22,000
If you're able to share what's an interesting story or engagement you've had with a DAO or protocol.

74
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Let's think.

75
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Interesting story with a DAO or protocol.

76
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Well, one fun example was actually a tax engagement we did for a few venture capital funds,

77
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:38,000
when DYDX, a popular perpetualist platform, was migrating from Ethereum to Cosmos.

78
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,000
That could have had some pretty large tax consequences if not managed properly.

79
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
So we did some work to help that.

80
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:51,000
These migrations are going to be interesting for you guys and for everyone beyond who is involved in those activities.

81
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
How do you view the broader stack of services for crypto firms?

82
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:01,000
I imagine that the clients that you're working with, they work with a host of other participants in other parts of the industry.

83
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
So obviously they need financial audits.

84
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
They also need smart contract audits.

85
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
There's traditional cybersecurity.

86
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Where do you guys see yourself sitting and how does your role evolve in the industry's progression?

87
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
The question.

88
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
So for one thing, I'd say when an independent public accounting firm uses the term audit,

89
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,000
it has a very specific definition and it doesn't always align with what the crypto industry calls an audit

90
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,000
in the terms of something like a smart contract audit, as you mentioned.

91
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:35,000
So we're definitely quite involved in the cybersecurity space more broadly as a firm.

92
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Things like traditional pen testing and vulnerability assessments,

93
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
governance engagements with actually gen AI governance being a really hot one these days.

94
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,000
I'd say the closest type of engagement we do that overlaps cybersecurity and crypto

95
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
revolves maybe around private key and custody management,

96
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
things like assessing or advising on key custody, key ceremonies.

97
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Regarding smart contract analysis, we occasionally do those types of activities

98
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:07,000
for certain audit procedures for firms, for example, that recognize material revenue from smart contracts.

99
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:13,000
There's also a level of smart contract analysis that is required in order for us to perform examinations

100
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
for issued stablecoins and related reserves, like I mentioned before.

101
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:23,000
That being said, we've done the risk and opportunity analysis and providing things like external smart contract attestations.

102
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,000
It's a service we haven't invested into date, but maybe one day.

103
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
That's a nice segue since it sounds like there are a lot of ways that you're acting with the firms you work with.

104
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
How is the attitude towards auditing of crypto firms you've engaged with evolved?

105
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,000
Well, audits are imperative for responsible capital formation in US markets

106
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and these crypto firms know that they owe it to their investors and stakeholders

107
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:52,000
to provide financial statements that can be relied on and that they have internal controls over financial reporting

108
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
that operates effectively for them.

109
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:59,000
So we're in the business of providing high quality audits that comply with our professional standards

110
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
that stand the test of time.

111
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And we perform a robust client acceptance process for all new audit clients, not just crypto companies.

112
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,000
As I mentioned earlier, we're proud to provide audit services to many of the leading companies in the industry

113
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
and we're keen to work with many more.

114
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
So our services to the crypto industry will continue to evolve as the industry evolves

115
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
and the legal and regulatory picture becomes increasingly clearer.

116
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
And maybe touch on that with a little bit more depth.

117
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:29,000
How is your firm and the crypto-facing audit industry evolved to date?

118
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:35,000
I'd say our firm has evolved because our clients needs change over time and as do the regulations.

119
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:40,000
The technology gets more advanced and new chains and L2s are popping up all the time,

120
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
their clients are engaging with.

121
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
So we need to make sure that we're adapting alongside that.

122
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Another great example is that regulation is evolving.

123
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
The Genius Act, for example, if it makes it all the way into signed law,

124
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
that requires monthly attestations by public accounting firms for dollar-pegged payment stablecoins.

125
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:02,000
And we're ahead of the game on that and it's because of how active we are both with listening to our clients' needs

126
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
as well as engaging with entities such as the AICPA.

127
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,000
And what's been the AICPA's approach towards crypto both in the past and fast-forward to today?

128
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,000
I'd say that AICPA deserves a ton of credit for its efforts to advance the conversation

129
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,000
and provide guidance on accounting and auditing matters that affect the crypto industry more broadly.

130
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:26,000
Back in 2018, the AICPA launched the first annual Blockchain and Accounting Symposium

131
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,000
convening Blockchain and Digital Assets Subject Matter Experts from CPA firms,

132
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
technology companies, and consulting entities.

133
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:37,000
The AICPA also established its Digital Assets Working Group in 2019

134
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:41,000
to develop accounting and auditing guides in the digital assets area.

135
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
It's a realm that we've been involved with.

136
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,000
It's published a popular practice aid on accounting for and auditing of digital assets,

137
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:50,000
and they've hosted a series of webcasts.

138
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
And then on the attestation side, actually just a couple months ago back in March,

139
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
the AICPA published the 2025 Criteria for Stablecoin Reporting,

140
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
specific to asset-backed fiat-pegged tokens,

141
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,000
which provides a common framework for stablecoin issuers to disclose information

142
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
about outstanding stablecoins and the availability of assets backing them.

143
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:15,000
Also, the AICPA is working on developing criteria for controls supporting stablecoin operations as well.

144
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:19,000
So, they've been a lot. They've always been very curious and very easy to work with.

145
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Yeah, it's great to hear about the stablecoin focus.

146
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
You touched on innovation a little bit.

147
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Do you feel that currently you and the firm are able to keep pace

148
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
with the innovation that we've seen in the space?

149
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
There's a lot happening every day, obviously.

150
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
And do you think the technologies and systems in place

151
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
to analyze the range of what you see in the market are there?

152
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Definitely. I would say we know what it takes to serve market leaders

153
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:45,000
and continue to make significant investments in tools, data,

154
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
and human capital to serve our clients.

155
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Our clients are always engaging in new chains, more complex transactions, DeFi.

156
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:58,000
And as a result, our processes, tools, and technologies, they're constantly expanding.

157
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
There are always going to be new chains and new fields you need to be on top of.

158
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,000
How has your work with crypto companies evolved in the face of regulatory shifts that we've seen?

159
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,000
So, one way our work evolves is when new guidance is issued,

160
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
for example, from entities such as FASB or the SEC.

161
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:19,000
So, when there are new standards set, both our clients and our sales have to stay on top of that

162
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,000
to verify if our clients are compliant with those new requirements.

163
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
We also monitor other regulatory shifts closely

164
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and the impact both how we think about client acceptance

165
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,000
as well as risk assessment and other audit procedures we perform with our clients.

166
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Are you seeing an uptick in crypto-related needs for your more traditional clients?

167
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Speaking on the clients, you mentioned earlier you're seeing an incorporation of stablecoins,

168
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
treasury strategies, or Bitcoin holdings.

169
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:49,000
How is that impacting what you're being asked for from your traditional clients?

170
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Great question.

171
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:55,000
So, I definitely say there's an uptick in curiosity and enthusiasm across the board.

172
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Part of my job is to provide insights to our many audit clients,

173
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
even those that are not native to the crypto industry,

174
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:07,000
about what disruptors we're seeing in technology and the corporate finance function more broadly.

175
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:13,000
So, I've been talking to CFOs, CAOs, controllers, tax directors, et cetera,

176
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:18,000
for years about the developments, implications, and benefits of blockchain technology

177
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
with a big focus on stablecoins.

178
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:24,000
So, one example of that have the largest ERP platforms are incorporating stablecoins

179
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,000
how the world's leading payments providers are leading into stablecoins.

180
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,000
It's really starting to click for people as an option they should consider for payments

181
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
and cross-border remittances.

182
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:38,000
As you might guess, the largest amount of active interest is coming from the fintech,

183
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,000
asset management, and banking industries.

184
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:45,000
So, there's definitely been major moves some of these entities have already made in the space,

185
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:50,000
but I expect the big unlock is going to come when there is fully legislated stablecoin

186
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,000
and market structure laws.

187
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,000
Banks are very risk averse just like us, just like public accounting firms,

188
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,000
and many are still waiting to see what happens with advancing legislation.

189
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:03,000
All of that being said, while we are definitely seeing a large increase in activity,

190
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,000
I'd still say most of our traditional clients have not meaningfully engaged in the ecosystem yet,

191
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,000
and as the old adage goes, we're still early.

192
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
Yeah, we're still early.

193
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
How have attitudes evolved in this respect?

194
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Because I probably imagine that the actual strategic moves to integrating crypto workflows

195
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
don't mirror where the attitudes stand.

196
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
There's usually a lag there.

197
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Are you seeing attitudes evolving in that respect among those traditional clients,

198
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
or do you still see a post-FTX crater of sorts?

199
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Do people view stablecoins markedly different from crypto these days,

200
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
speaking of stablecoins?

201
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
So, I find that people don't know what they don't know.

202
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,000
When we schedule these sessions with our many audit clients to see what topics they want to hear about,

203
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:49,000
blockchain and crypto and digital assets, it's generally not high on that list,

204
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:54,000
but when we do make our way into talking about it, it's always one of the best received topics.

205
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
And again, they just don't know what they don't know.

206
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
Regarding what you mentioned about that post-FTX crater there,

207
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:04,000
I'd say we're almost fully past the post-FTX at this point

208
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:09,000
where it would really put a damper on the attitude towards blockchain for a while.

209
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Even during that really grueling bear market, we actually continue to invest in our capabilities

210
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:19,000
because we believe in the promise and ethos of the technology along with our clients.

211
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
We didn't abandon them during all of that and they didn't abandon us

212
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
and growth across the board is picking up and interest again,

213
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
is picking up especially with the regulatory changes here.

214
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:34,000
Regarding how people differentiate and conflate crypto to blockchain to Bitcoin to stablecoins,

215
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:39,000
we still have a long ways to go, I'd say, before the masses are educated on those nuances,

216
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
but it is heading in the right direction.

217
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:45,000
That's good to hear about the subject matter interests when you guys are touching on that.

218
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:49,000
What are the blockers or potential unlocks to some of those parties that you mentioned

219
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
to launching crypto-powered products or workflows?

220
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Especially for more of the traditional companies, first they would need a real business need.

221
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
So what does crypto or blockchain solve for them?

222
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:05,000
Most eyes are on efficiencies gained through disintermediation of the transfer of assets

223
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
and I'd say most large entities in the asset management area

224
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,000
are actively looking at this narrative of real-world asset tokenization.

225
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:17,000
It's also hard to ignore everything that the payments giants are doing in this space

226
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
and how that could innovate on settlement and payments.

227
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,000
Very cool.

228
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:25,000
When it comes to these use cases, payments being a chief one,

229
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
how do you feel about permission versus public blockchains?

230
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Yeah, I get that question a lot.

231
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,000
So this is definitely a Greg opinion, not necessarily a firm opinion here,

232
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
so I want to be sure to clarify that.

233
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
But I personally believe there are a lot of powerful attributes

234
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
that are inherent in blockchain technology.

235
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:44,000
But I think the most important attribute that gives these systems value

236
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
is censorship resistance.

237
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:50,000
With public blockchains, the fact that all you need is an internet connection

238
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:54,000
and you have access to a global financial system with no biases

239
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and no counterparty risk, that's a very powerful and valuable thing.

240
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
Public blockchains, I feel, are effectively public goods

241
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and when you talk about permissioned blockchains,

242
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:08,000
you're effectively removing that very key attribute of censorship resistance

243
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:12,000
right out of the gate and maybe that causes to question the value proposition

244
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
of the system compared to a traditional centralized system

245
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:19,000
based on your typical servers, databases, APIs, etc.

246
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
Now, that being said, there are some popular permissioned blockchains

247
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:26,000
used by some of the world's largest banks and asset managers

248
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:29,000
that I would actually say are a resounding success

249
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:33,000
and I would agree they're providing an important and valuable service.

250
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,000
But I think this was because a result of regulatory entities

251
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:40,000
historically calling into question if public blockchains instill

252
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
safety and sound as concerns in the banking sector.

253
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,000
You take that and combine it with the fact that it's also really difficult

254
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:51,000
and expensive to create a new system of network, servers, databases

255
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
to execute financial transactions across.

256
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
And the software underpinning the Ethereum blockchain,

257
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,000
try true and tested for executing low settlement financial transactions.

258
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
And since that software can be effectively copy, fit for purpose

259
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
and ran in a permissioned environment,

260
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,000
that might be a much easier, less complicated option

261
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,000
than creating a parallel and alternate financial system

262
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
that you have to get industry players to agree on.

263
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
So long story short, I think they both have a time and place

264
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
depending on the facts and circumstances,

265
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:21,000
but public blockchains are resoundingly more imperative systems.

266
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
Yeah, it's a great point.

267
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
It's not easy to stand up a blockchain,

268
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,000
both to get it working and then to actually get the flywheel going.

269
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:33,000
I think that's a lot of why you see these token incentive methods employed.

270
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
Granted, it's potentially different scope.

271
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
Since like you said, that was maybe a personal view,

272
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
but how does this impact your guy's job?

273
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:43,000
KPMG's role that permission versus public blockchain dichotomy.

274
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Well, auditing ledgers is our thing.

275
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Permissioned or public, they have different tradeoffs

276
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
and efficiencies and how we audit them.

277
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:52,000
They have slightly different processes that we might utilize,

278
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
but they're structured pretty well.

279
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:58,000
And for the public chains, we have our proprietary set of tools for that.

280
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
And even for the permission chains,

281
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
we always have a way to get and correlate that data

282
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
and their internal controls and such.

283
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
And in a similar vein, how about when it comes to permissioned

284
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:12,000
or KYC'd assets on chain versus permissionlessly transferable ones?

285
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
I like where you're heading with this question.

286
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
So we're actually seeing a lot of recent innovations more broadly

287
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:21,000
in the crypto space, such as with Uniswap v4 transfer hooks

288
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:25,000
and Solana token extensions as part of the token 2022 standard.

289
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:28,000
So for those that don't know, these are effectively smart contracts

290
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:31,000
that exist on public permissionless chains

291
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:34,000
that allow for KYC processes and procedures

292
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:38,000
that won't need to comply with before something like a transfer function

293
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:41,000
or any smart contract can be called on that asset.

294
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:44,000
And I think that's a super elegant way to address KYC concerns

295
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
while still keeping that base settlement layer

296
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
of a public blockchain permissionless.

297
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
And on top of this, there's also a lot of ZK

298
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,000
or zero knowledge-based proofs that can be combined with these methods

299
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:58,000
to instill more privacy alongside those KYC controls.

300
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:02,000
So as the value of tokenization and tokenized real-world assets keeps growing,

301
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
I think these advancements are a really logical place

302
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
to build those solutions upon.

303
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:10,000
Yeah, it's exciting to see crypto become somewhat natively compliant,

304
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:14,000
having those technologies in place to just make these transfers

305
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:17,000
more comfortable for real institutional-grade users.

306
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
Are there any frequent flags that come up

307
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
when specifically auditing crypto companies

308
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
or anything to highlight in that regard?

309
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:27,000
I don't know if I would necessarily characterize these as flags,

310
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:31,000
but I could think of a few issues that we think are important to work through.

311
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:34,000
So how a client is able to demonstrate existence

312
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:38,000
and writes the digital assets when they are held with a third party,

313
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:41,000
particularly when the third party does not have something like a SOC report

314
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:44,000
that provides assurance over its internal controls,

315
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
that's a really important issue to work through.

316
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:49,000
Also, the client's own internal controls over financial reporting,

317
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:54,000
particularly those that address the completeness and accuracy of financial information,

318
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
related party transactions,

319
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:59,000
particularly given the pseudonymous nature of many blockchains.

320
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:03,000
And of course, we always see the company that has too many spreadsheets.

321
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:06,000
I have a colleague that has said before that often the digital assets

322
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
aren't the riskiest part of the audit,

323
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
but rather a client's use of spreadsheets

324
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,000
because of all the things that can go wrong with them without robust controls.

325
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:17,000
That makes sense. I buy it 100%.

326
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:22,000
Greg, it's been great learning about you and the firm's involvement and thoughtful directions.

327
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
In closing, I want to ask what guidance would you give to founders

328
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
just getting started building crypto businesses?

329
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:31,000
Early on, I'd say most of the focus needs to be on building something

330
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
that has product market fit,

331
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:37,000
something where you're providing a product or service that others find valuable

332
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
and worth paying for.

333
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:42,000
And once you have that idea, maybe a prototype, MVP, etc.,

334
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:46,000
then I think it's important that companies start thinking real early

335
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:50,000
about the various challenges they can run into that could threaten their business,

336
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
hinder growth, or instill too much risk.

337
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:57,000
I think companies should be sure to document everything that they can in a secure fashion,

338
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:00,000
focus on things like SOC-ready IT security,

339
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:04,000
bulletproof KYC and AML processes, and sanctions controls,

340
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,000
and of course, don't cheap out on private key control and custody.

341
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,000
So the earlier that companies can start thinking about these things,

342
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:14,000
the easier it's going to get down the line when they become a larger focus

343
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
as a scalar business.

344
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:17,000
We'll leave it there.

345
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:20,000
Well, Greg, thank you for coming on and sharing you and the firm's insights.

346
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
It's been great to hear about.

347
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:23,000
Yeah, it's been a pleasure.

348
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
Thanks so much for having me.

349
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
I appreciate it's been fun.

